well Reid Hoffman serial entrepreneur  tech investor isn't called the startup  whisperer  of Silicon Valley from PayPal to  LinkedIn to Facebook he's had his hand  in so many of Tech's most successful  companies well Bloomberg West's Emily  Chang sat down with Hoffman in her new  series studio 1.0 and Emily what did you  discover that we didn't know about Reid  Hoffman hey there Betty well studio 1.0  is really an opportunity to go in depth  with the influencers in tech and media  we talked to these guys all the time on  Bloomberg West when they're making news  but we don't talk to them about who they  are how they got there and where they're  going and that's what we did with Reid  Hoffman Betty he's one of the most  decorated guys in tech from PayPal to  LinkedIn he's now a venture capitalist  at Greylock he's an angel and personal  investor in dozens of companies he's on  the board of seven companies and now  he's the author of not one but two books  he's out with a new book next week  called the Alliance it's about  rethinking the relationship between  employer and employee take a listen to  how he describes it the key thing is in  many circumstances companies employers  and employees are lying to each other  because the notion is the modern career  is actually in fact changing where  there's options for shifting companies  for how do you evolve even career tracks  are changing the question is how do you  get that investment in the future and  that entrepreneurial relationship  between the company the employer and the  employee and the short answer is  eyelines and that's an alliance is based  on tours of duty where you set up for a  project that's like three or five years  long and that transforms your career  makes you employable and transforms the  company  so the idea Betty is a job isn't forever  and you know neither party should be  under the impression that it is but it  should be advancing your career in some  way and this isn't a book just for the  Silicon Valley set this is something  that he hopes really every middle middle  manager in America is going to want to  read it's actually a pretty good read  Betty and I found it interesting and you  know I think any manager would find it  interesting and useful to Emily what was  the most surprising thing that you heard  from read so many surprising things I  mean we talked to him  about everything from growing up in  Berkeley to being obsessed with Dungeons  and Dragons but I think one of the most  surprising and interesting parts of the  interview was when he talked about  PayPal the PayPal mafia as they are  known in Silicon Valley and now beyond  you know all of the people who came  together to found and start PayPal was  really a group of a bunch of people who  were all entrepreneurial themselves and  they went on to start companies like  Tesla SpaceX Yammer Yelp LinkedIn all of  these company YouTube all of these  companies founded by members of the  PayPal mafia so I asked him what was  special about the members of that family  the people who started that company he  said all of them really had their own  sort of entrepreneurial spirit and even  though they worked well together at  PayPal they all went on to start their  own things and one of the most  interesting things he said is that they  all still call each other all the time  when he's talking about financial  modeling or needs help with complicated  financial things Reid Hoffman calls  Peter Thiel when he's thinking about big  data and business models with respect to  technology he calls Max Levchin when  he's thinking about you know crazy big  ideas where risk is not a factor he  calls Elon Musk and I thought that that  was that was really fascinating that  indeed the PayPal mafia is very much  still alive and well in Silicon Valley
 

